1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,076
For centuries, the world called this
country Persia.

2
00:00:03,316 --> 00:00:06,162
Then, in 1935, its king asked foreign

3
00:00:06,182 --> 00:00:09,416
governments to use a different name: Iran.

4
00:00:09,656 --> 00:00:14,312
He picked one date for the change. March
21. The first day of spring.

5
00:00:14,552 --> 00:00:18,180
Why that day? To find out, you need three
things:

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:22,125
an empire, a poem, and a table set with
seven things.

7
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:27,065
This is Iran. Nearly 90 million people
live here. The capital is Tehran.

8
00:00:27,305 --> 00:00:30,763
First, the empire. Around 550 BC, a king

9
00:00:30,783 --> 00:00:34,155
named Cyrus rose in Pars, in the south.

10
00:00:34,395 --> 00:00:36,632
The Greeks called that region Persis.

11
00:00:36,652 --> 00:00:39,865
The Romans turned it into Persia, and the
name stuck.

12
00:00:40,105 --> 00:00:45,705
By Guinness's count, around 480 BC this
empire ruled 44% of all people on Earth.

13
00:00:45,945 --> 00:00:49,987
Its ceremonial capital, Persepolis, still
stands near Shiraz.

14
00:00:50,007 --> 00:00:54,515
On its great staircase, 23 nations march
up with gifts for the king.

15
00:00:54,755 --> 00:00:57,106
But Persia was the name of just one
province.

16
00:00:57,126 --> 00:01:00,794
Inside the country, people used another
word for the whole land: Iran.

17
00:01:01,034 --> 00:01:06,379
It is carved in stone on a cliff near
Persepolis, in the 3rd century AD.

18
00:01:06,399 --> 00:01:09,359
So the name is at least 1,700 years old.

19
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:12,499
So how did the name survive? Because the
empire did not.

20
00:01:12,739 --> 00:01:16,271
In 651, Arab armies ended the last Persian
empire, and

21
00:01:16,291 --> 00:01:19,559
much of the old culture was at risk of
being lost.

22
00:01:19,799 --> 00:01:24,446
Three centuries later, a poet named
Ferdowsi spent about 30 years on one book.

23
00:01:24,686 --> 00:01:28,436
The Shahnameh, the Book of Kings, has
50,000 rhyming couplets.

24
00:01:28,456 --> 00:01:31,780
It is the longest epic poem written by a
single author.

25
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:35,314
Scholars credit it with keeping the
Persian language alive.

26
00:01:35,554 --> 00:01:38,917
An empire, and a poem. The third thing
explains the date.

27
00:01:38,937 --> 00:01:42,062
But first, two practical things, in case
you ever go.

28
00:01:42,302 --> 00:01:46,345
Money. $10 is about 27 million rials, on
the open market.

29
00:01:46,365 --> 00:01:49,980
The rial is falling fast, so always check
the rate.

30
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:55,363
For $10, a metro ride costs about 10
cents. A cheap lunch, around $3.50.

31
00:01:55,383 --> 00:01:58,590
A short ride in a ride-hailing app, $1 to
$4.

32
00:01:58,830 --> 00:02:02,745
A dinner for two in a mid-range
restaurant? About $17.

33
00:02:02,765 --> 00:02:04,858
So $10 covers most of a meal.

34
00:02:05,098 --> 00:02:06,318
Now, the don't list.

35
00:02:06,558 --> 00:02:09,938
Don't photograph government buildings,
police, or military sites.

36
00:02:10,178 --> 00:02:14,248
Don't count on cards. Foreign cards don't
work in Iran, so bring cash.

37
00:02:14,488 --> 00:02:18,280
Don't ignore the dress code. It applies to
visitors, starting at the airport.

38
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:22,819
Don't carry alcohol or drugs. Both are
illegal, even for tourists.

39
00:02:23,059 --> 00:02:27,360
And don't take a driver's first offer of a
free ride. That's taarof.

40
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:29,584
He expects you to insist on paying.

41
00:02:29,824 --> 00:02:32,836
Now, the third thing. Nowruz means new
day.

42
00:02:32,856 --> 00:02:37,702
It begins at the exact moment of the
spring equinox, around March 21.

43
00:02:37,942 --> 00:02:42,873
It is more than 3,000 years old, and
UNESCO listed it in 2009.

44
00:02:43,113 --> 00:02:45,748
First, families clean the whole house.

45
00:02:45,768 --> 00:02:49,242
On the last Tuesday night of the year,
people jump

46
00:02:49,262 --> 00:02:53,505
over small bonfires, to leave the old
year's bad luck behind.

47
00:02:53,745 --> 00:02:56,419
Then comes the table. The haft-seen:

48
00:02:56,439 --> 00:03:00,385
seven things whose names all start with
the letter S.

49
00:03:00,405 --> 00:03:03,827
Sprouts, an apple, garlic, vinegar, and
sumac.

50
00:03:04,067 --> 00:03:07,978
Each one is a wish. Sprouts for rebirth.
An apple for beauty. Garlic for health.

51
00:03:08,218 --> 00:03:09,839
The new year lasts 13 days.

52
00:03:09,859 --> 00:03:13,729
On the 13th, almost the whole country goes
outside for a picnic.

53
00:03:13,969 --> 00:03:16,771
Now you can see why the king chose March
21.

54
00:03:16,791 --> 00:03:19,465
A name Iranians had used for 17 centuries,

55
00:03:19,485 --> 00:03:22,223
asked of the world on their oldest
holiday.

56
00:03:22,463 --> 00:03:27,754
Did the world obey? Not entirely. In 1959,
his son said both names were fine.

57
00:03:27,774 --> 00:03:29,754
We still say Persian carpets.

58
00:03:29,994 --> 00:03:33,854
That was Iran. Which country should we
visit next? Tell me in the comments.

